WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.257
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.557 --> 00:00:09.118
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.418 --> 00:00:15.994
Unit eight: Economic History. Today you will explain historical cause and effect with 'led to' and 'resulted in'.

4
00:00:16.294 --> 00:00:18.993
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.933 --> 00:00:22.922
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.783 --> 00:00:25.202
Industrialization.

7
00:00:26.217 --> 00:00:30.469
Industrialization means the development of industries on a large scale.

8
00:00:31.083 --> 00:00:33.803
Industrialization led to rapid urban growth.

9
00:00:34.417 --> 00:00:37.812
Your turn. Say: Industrialization.

10
00:00:40.667 --> 00:00:42.132
Industrialization.

11
00:00:42.883 --> 00:00:43.815
Trade route.

12
00:00:44.817 --> 00:00:47.629
Trade route means a path used regularly for trade.

13
00:00:48.217 --> 00:00:50.657
The Silk Road was a major trade route.

14
00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:53.972
Your turn. Say: Trade route.

15
00:00:56.833 --> 00:00:57.951
Trade route.

16
00:00:58.717 --> 00:00:59.683
Currency.

17
00:01:00.683 --> 00:01:03.600
Currency means the system of money used in a country.

18
00:01:04.217 --> 00:01:08.608
The riel was reintroduced as Cambodia's currency in 1980.

19
00:01:09.217 --> 00:01:11.799
Your turn. Say: Currency.

20
00:01:14.633 --> 00:01:15.855
Currency.

21
00:01:16.617 --> 00:01:17.943
Market economy.

22
00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:23.016
Market economy means an economy where prices are set by supply and demand.

23
00:01:23.617 --> 00:01:27.648
Cambodia moved to a market economy in the 1990s.

24
00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:31.285
Your turn. Say: Market economy.

25
00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:35.494
Market economy.

26
00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:37.193
Recession.

27
00:01:38.183 --> 00:01:40.729
Recession means a period of economic decline.

28
00:01:41.317 --> 00:01:45.023
The 1930s recession caused mass unemployment.

29
00:01:45.617 --> 00:01:48.304
Your turn. Say: Recession.

30
00:01:51.167 --> 00:01:52.145
Recession.

31
00:01:52.883 --> 00:01:53.641
Growth.

32
00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:57.265
Growth means an increase in economic output.

33
00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:00.581
Garment exports drove growth for two decades.

34
00:02:01.183 --> 00:02:03.453
Your turn. Say: Growth.

35
00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:07.046
Growth.

36
00:02:07.783 --> 00:02:08.715
Labor.

37
00:02:09.717 --> 00:02:12.181
Labor means work, or the people who do it.

38
00:02:12.783 --> 00:02:15.108
Cheap labor attracted foreign factories.

39
00:02:15.717 --> 00:02:17.905
Your turn. Say: Labor.

40
00:02:20.767 --> 00:02:21.617
Labor.

41
00:02:22.383 --> 00:02:23.338
Capital.

42
00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:27.116
Capital means money or assets used to produce wealth.

43
00:02:27.717 --> 00:02:30.343
Factories required large amounts of capital.

44
00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:33.405
Your turn. Say: Capital.

45
00:02:36.267 --> 00:02:37.128
Capital.

46
00:02:37.933 --> 00:02:41.909
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:47.164
Visal, how did the Industrial Revolution change ordinary people's lives?

48
00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:58.786
Industrialization led to rapid urban growth. Millions moved from farms to factories, which resulted in crowded cities and new social classes.

49
00:02:59.883 --> 00:03:01.767
Was it good or bad for workers?

50
00:03:02.883 --> 00:03:14.394
In the short term, long hours and child labor resulted in great suffering. In the long run, rising productivity led to higher living standards.

51
00:03:15.483 --> 00:03:18.098
Do you see similar patterns in Cambodia?

52
00:03:19.183 --> 00:03:31.379
Yes. The growth of garment factories has led to migration from villages to Phnom Penh, and it has given young women new income but hard conditions.

53
00:03:32.533 --> 00:03:34.336
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:35.183 --> 00:03:39.203
Grammar focus: Cause-effect: led to and resulted in.

55
00:03:39.503 --> 00:03:40.678
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:41.383 --> 00:03:44.288
Industrialization led to rapid urban growth.

57
00:03:45.183 --> 00:03:48.425
Long hours and child labor resulted in great suffering.

58
00:03:49.317 --> 00:03:52.767
Rising productivity gave rise to higher living standards.

59
00:03:53.733 --> 00:04:05.386
'Lead to / result in / give rise to' + noun: cause → effect. Reverse the direction with 'result from' or 'stem from': 'Urban growth resulted from industrialization.' Never say 'resulted to'.

60
00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:10.091
Be careful! Don't say: Industrialization resulted to growth.

61
00:04:12.033 --> 00:04:14.869
Say: Industrialization resulted in growth.

62
00:04:15.169 --> 00:04:17.296
Say 'result in', not 'result to'.

63
00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:20.532
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:21.333 --> 00:04:23.194
How Economies Transform.

65
00:04:23.494 --> 00:04:29.522
Economic history examines how changes in production, trade and money have reshaped human societies.

66
00:04:30.533 --> 00:04:36.086
The opening of maritime trade routes led to an unprecedented exchange of goods between continents.

67
00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:42.617
Later, industrialization in Britain resulted in a dramatic shift from agricultural to factory labor.

68
00:04:43.633 --> 00:04:49.093
Such transformations also gave rise to new conflicts between owners of capital and workers.

69
00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:56.836
Studying these patterns helps economists and policymakers anticipate the effects of today's globalised economy.

70
00:04:57.933 --> 00:05:02.063
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:02.917 --> 00:05:05.613
The drought blank a sharp rise in food prices.

72
00:05:05.913 --> 00:05:10.681
A, resulted in. B, resulted to. C, resulted from.

73
00:05:14.233 --> 00:05:16.361
The answer is A: resulted in.

74
00:05:17.517 --> 00:05:20.050
The crisis blank poor bank regulation.

75
00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:24.561
A, led to. B, resulted from. C, gave rise.

76
00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:30.448
The answer is B: resulted from.

77
00:05:31.583 --> 00:05:34.175
Money or assets used to produce wealth is blank.

78
00:05:34.475 --> 00:05:37.931
A, labor. B, currency. C, capital.

79
00:05:41.467 --> 00:05:43.176
The answer is C: capital.

80
00:05:44.317 --> 00:05:46.235
Which collocation is correct?

81
00:05:46.535 --> 00:05:51.616
A, trigger a recession. B, do a recession. C, open a recession.

82
00:05:55.167 --> 00:05:57.538
The answer is A: trigger a recession.

83
00:05:58.683 --> 00:06:01.763
According to the reading, industrialization resulted in…

84
00:06:02.063 --> 00:06:07.980
A, a shift from farm to factory labor. B, the end of trade. C, a fall in production.

85
00:06:11.533 --> 00:06:15.286
The answer is A: a shift from farm to factory labor.

86
00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:19.744
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:20.583 --> 00:06:23.512
How did industrialization change the way people worked?

88
00:06:40.317 --> 00:06:41.689
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:41.989 --> 00:07:17.343
Industrialization changed work by moving production from homes and farms into large factories. Work was organised around machines and clocks, which led to long, controlled hours. For example, families who had once worked together in the fields now worked separately in mills. Although early factory conditions resulted in great hardship, the increase in productivity eventually raised living standards. Today, Cambodia's garment industry shows similar benefits and challenges, so these lessons remain very relevant.

90
00:07:17.643 --> 00:07:19.840
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:26.107
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and cause-effect: led to and resulted in.

92
00:07:26.407 --> 00:07:31.672
Today's value is economic literacy. Understand how money and work shape people's lives.

93
00:07:31.972 --> 00:07:34.404
See you in Unit nine. Goodbye!

94
00:07:34.704 --> 00:07:37.704
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

